unt', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\n2: 1, A: 1, Q: 1, J: 2, 10: 2, 9: 1, 8: 3, 7: 3, 6: 4, 5: 2, 4: 1, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\npair: ♠Q ♣Q\npair: ♥K ♣K\npair: ♥2 ♦2\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**Strategic Analysis: Dou Dizhu - Peasant Perspective**\n\nAlright, here\'s the situation: I\'m the downstream peasant, the gatekeeper in this game. The Landlord just tossed out a pair of 5s – *very* low play. My gut reaction is to crush that with a high pair, prevent him from gaining any momentum. Looking at my hand, I\'ve got three options for pairs: Qs, Ks, or 2s. Passing isn\'t a good choice because it\'s the gatekeeper\'s job to control the game.\n\nThe Landlord has 9 cards. The teammate has 14. This means I\'m supposed to try and control. If I let the Landlord have it, he gets the lead again. I need to make the right move, which is to play a pair. I\'ve got a lot of pairs. I should use them. Now, playing my 2s would be overkill, burning my highest card immediately. K is almost as strong as 2. I have to save those cards.\n\nMy cards are: ♠7 ♣8 ♣9 ♠Q ♣Q ♥K ♣K ♥2 ♦2. I\'m thinking, "Should I go with the Qs? The K? Or save the 2s?"\n\nConsidering the flow of the game, the Landlord\'s play of the 5s indicates he\'s either clearing out low cards or probing for an opportunity to regain control. The key is to force out high cards. I have the power cards.\n\nMy singles are weak (7, 8, 9). They are useless by themselves. The pairs are the key to this hand: Queen, King, and the deuces.\n\nBased on the card history, the teammate played a Queen, so it\'s likely he doesn\'t have a pair of Queens left. I can take that opportunity!\n\nLooking at the card unknowns: 2: 1, A: 1, Q: 1. I\'ve got 2 of them. It seems a total of 1 card for each exists. Aces, Queens, and the deuces are quite valuable.\n\nHere is the strategy: If I play the Q pair now, I can prevent the landlord from leading again. Also, I\'ve got a single 7, 8, and 9 to follow up. It will be helpful to establish some card controls. Playing my Queens is a strong way to force the landlord to play out his hand.\nIf the Landlord plays a higher pair, great. I saved my King pair and Deuce pair.\nIf he doesn\'t play the high cards? It is a waste, but it\'s a good waste because I can then follow up with high cards on my turn.\n\nSo, I\'m going with **playing the pair of Queens (♠Q ♣Q)**. It\'s the most logical move to block the Landlord and it preserves my high control cards (K, 2) for later. It\'s the standard peasant play to block the landlord! I\'m going to follow the established strategy. It fits the situation very well!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has played a low pair (5s) to test the water or clear small cards. As the Peasant playing immediately after the Landlord (the "Gatekeeper" position), it is my responsibility to block these small plays to prevent the Landlord from gaining easy momentum or shedding trash cards. I hold three strong pairs (Q, K, 2). Playing the Pair of Queens is the optimal response: it is strong enough to force the Landlord to use high cards (like Aces or 2s) or yield the lead, yet it reserves my Kings and 2s for maintaining control later in the round. Passing would leave the burden to my teammate, who holds a large hand (14 cards) and may not be positioned to defend.\n\nReason: My hand structure is pair-heavy (Q, K, 2) with weak singles (7, 8, 9). To win, I need to use my strength (pairs) to block the Landlord and eventually find an opportunity to play my singles or force a situation where I can clear them. Blocking the Landlord\'s Pair 5s with Pair Qs exerts pressure on the Landlord (who has 9 cards left) without wasting